-- title:
--   The maximum norm is attained at a coordinate
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$ (formally, $\{1,\dots,d\}$ is nonempty) and let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$. The maximum norm is defined as the supremum of the absolute values of the coordinates,
--
--   $$\lVert x \rVert_\infty = \sup_{i} \lvert x_i \rvert ,$$
--
--   and the claim is that this supremum is attained: there is an index $i$ with
--
--   $$\lVert x \rVert_\infty = \lvert x_i \rvert .$$
--
--   Since the index set is finite and nonempty, the supremum is a maximum; the statement records this so that arguments about $\lVert \cdot \rVert_\infty$ may work with a witnessing coordinate rather than with a supremum. This is the standard entry point for comparing the maximum norm with the Euclidean norm and for the cube geometry built on $\lVert\cdot\rVert_\infty$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $\lVert x\rVert_\infty$ is defined as `⨆ i, |x i|`, a `Real` indexed supremum, which in Lean would return the junk value $0$ for an empty index type; the hypothesis `Nonempty (Fin d)` is exactly what rules that out. The ambient space is `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`, so the *ambient* norm is the $\ell^2$ one and $\lVert\cdot\rVert_\infty$ is a separately defined function on it, not the space's norm.
